{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And they set over him, etc.</b> Here the student asks: what wisdom did they devise in this? This is not wisdom but evil and brazenness. Further, how was their heart filled at the beginning of the servitude to set taskmasters over them? And further, how did the children of Israel turn their necks to labor at foreign slave-labor in mortar and bricks? And behold, our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sotah 11a) that the matter came by gradual progression — at first with soft speech and in the end with harshness — and from the order of the verses it appears that at first they set taskmasters over them, and according to their words, of blessed memory, one must say that things preceded the appointment of taskmasters." "Now, according to what I explained in the verse ״רַב וְעָצוּם מִמֶּנּוּ״ — that they thought they could enslave them in exchange for the good they did for them during the two years of famine — it is possible that they came upon them with force and might immediately, and enslaved them and set officers over them, and at that time they were few in comparison to the Egyptians and turned their necks. And what the verse says, ״הָבָה נִתְחַכְּמָה״ — it is not about the detail of the enslavement but about another detail, and so too they said of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 1:18) that it was concerning the drowning of the children in the river, as stated in their words." "<b>Further,</b> it appears upon close examination that it further says <b>לְמַעַן עַנֹּתוֹ</b> etc., which was unnecessary, for the matter is self-evident that their intent was לְמַעַן עַנּוֹתוֹ. It further says <b>בְּסִבְלֹתָם</b> — what does this come to diminish?" "Indeed, it appears that they devised against them to enslave them in this manner: for Pharaoh, in ordinary course, would have builders of cities for dwelling and many laborers performing the tasks needed for the kingdom from among the Egyptians themselves and from the rest; and those men are called ״מַס״ as the verse says (I Kings 5:27): ״וַיַּעַל הַמֶּלֶךְ שְׁלֹמֹה מַס מִכָּל יִשְׂרָאֵל״, and he took counsel to set over Israel that they should be the taskmasters — meaning, they would be the ones appointed over all who do the king's work. And this matter is not degradation nor poverty for Israel, and they cannot refuse. And their intent in this was cunning — לְמַעַן עַנּוֹתוֹ, to afflict the people of Israel with the burdens of the corvée laborers who work slave-labor: when they would not be able to perform the labor imposed upon them in far greater measure than they could bear, the officers would have to join with them to perform their labor to complete their quota, for no one knew the regulation of the labor except the officers, who were Israelites, who were the appointed ones. And this is their wisdom: through the device of this cunning they brought them into the burdens. And this is the statement of our Rabbis of blessed memory — at first, with soft speech, they were officers, and afterward, slaves; ״וַיִּבֶן עָרֵי״ etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {1 "The verse referenced is Exodus 1:9; the commentator cites it by its distinctive phrase rather than by address."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:23:13.172067295Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}